[h1]build83 - the ordinary Mystical Orbs now pay armour, not just weapons[/h1]

A database-only update. No map, quest or text change, so an existing character is unaffected and no
new game is needed.

[h2]What was wrong[/h2]

An earlier update taught every Mystical Orb to pay all six weapon classes, spear included. It widened
only the WEAPON row. A second update gave the mod's own chests and the apex orb a matching ARMOUR row,
but it decided which loot tables it owned by asking what folder they lived in, and fifteen orb tables
live somewhere else.

So nobody owned the armour on those fifteen, no audit looked at them, and both of the build's fail-loud
loot checks stayed green for three releases while fifteen live drop tables quietly starved. The ordinary
uber orbs, the level-banded ones most ubers drop, and Charon's orb were running between 3.4 and 8.5
weapons for every armour piece, with the thinnest worn slot paying as little as 0.007 pieces per open.
In practice that is a helm roughly once every 140 orbs.

[h2]What changed[/h2]

All fifteen now get the same armour treatment the apex orb has shipped with since build75:

[list]
[*]Weapons to armour moves from 3.41 to 8.47 down to 0.28 to 0.49, matching the apex orb exactly.
[*]The thinnest worn slot moves from 0.007 to 0.041 per open up to 0.29 to 1.16 per open.
[*]Every worn slot is covered: head, torso, arms, legs and shield.
[*]Total items per orb open goes UP, not sideways: 7.93 to 9.24, 9.32 to 11.77, 13.68 to 15.83.
[/list]

Nothing was reduced anywhere. Across the whole change, 165 values were raised, 15 members were added,
and zero values were lowered or replaced. The orb pools also grew rather than shrank.

[h2]The class of bug is gone, not just this one[/h2]

Ownership no longer depends on what folder a loot table sits in. The distribution check now derives the
orb tables from the orb feature's own scope, and it enforces a single rule: every loot table any module
writes must be inside the audited surface set. Two independent witnesses back it, and a missing witness
is announced rather than quietly passing. A future feature cannot add a loot table that nobody audits.

[h2]One honest note[/h2]

On these fifteen surfaces the mix now reads armour-heavy, roughly 2 to 3.5 armour pieces per weapon.
That is the exact ratio the apex orb has been shipping for several releases, so it is consistent rather
than invented, but it is a real change in feel and worth knowing about before you open one.

[h2]Verification[/h2]

Built twice from the committed source with byte-identical output. The change touches exactly 15 records
out of 51,253 and nothing else in the database moved by one byte. Every existing gate still passes on
the uploaded files, including the loot distribution, orb breadth, chest breadth, craft and thrown,
relic tier and Immortal Throne campaign cap checks, and the contract suite reports the same count as the
previous release, so this update adds zero new violations.
